---
description: Visual quality bar — it must feel like a 2D mobile game, not a website
alwaysApply: true
---

- Banned in gameplay: static answer cards, plain buttons, numeric input
  boxes, emoji-as-art, motionless panels, instant state swaps.
- Required: full-screen animated cartoon pirate excursion with constant ambient motion (waves,
  wake, sails, clouds, bobbing ships), chunky outlined shapes and warm storybook sea colours.
- Reserve cyan/magenta neon, slot ticks and casino lighting primarily for loot-box/jackpot
  ceremonies and brief high-stakes reveal peaks. Contrast makes rewards exciting.
- Text uses the fonts wired in the active client: Lilita One for display and Nunito 700/900
  for readable body/answers, unless an explicit typography migration changes both docs/code.
- Placeholder art must look intentional: thick outlines, gloss highlights,
  consistent palette — never developer-grey boxes.
- Answer text must stay readable at a glance on every island; if art fights
  readability, readability wins (scrims, plaques, strokes).
- Any React overlay that appears during gameplay must be styled like game HUD
  (chunky, glowing, on-palette), not like a settings form. The debug panel is
  the only exception.
- Gameplay actions should read as pirate-world cause/effect: a ship sails, cannon fires, flag
  rises or creature attacks. A generic card animation is not enough.
- Follow `docs/context/VISUAL_DIRECTION.md`.
